WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.338
Choose storage according to persistence and access patterns.

00:00:03.338 --> 00:00:07.600
An instance's ephemeral disk may provide fast temporary workspace but can disappear when

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:08.680
the instance is deleted.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.150
Persistent block disks survive independently and are useful for reusable environments,

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:14.420
caches, and active data.

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.720
Object storage supports durable datasets, model artifacts, and results at scale,

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.835
but applications may need to stage files locally for performance.

00:00:21.835 --> 00:00:24.975
Model caches reduce repeated downloads and provisioning time,

00:00:24.975 --> 00:00:28.660
yet they generate storage charges and may contain multiple large versions.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:32.200
Define which artifacts are authoritative, how their integrity is verified,

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.840
and when old cache entries are removed.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.500
Snapshots simplify recovery,

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.422
but uncontrolled snapshot growth creates both cost and data-retention risk.

00:00:39.422 --> 00:00:42.080
Back up configuration and irreplaceable data,

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.265
then test restoration onto a clean environment.

00:00:44.265 --> 00:00:48.580
Classify datasets, prompts, outputs, embeddings, and logs before uploading them.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:52.260
Prompts can contain credentials, source code, personal data, regulated records,

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.245
or business plans.

00:00:53.245 --> 00:00:56.140
Select approved accounts and regions, restrict access,

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:00.065
encrypt data in transit and at rest, and understand who controls encryption keys.

00:01:00.065 --> 00:01:04.250
Minimize collection and avoid logging complete prompts by default when operational metrics

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:05.140
would suffice.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.660
Define retention and deletion for every data class.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:12.140
Deleting a compute instance does not necessarily delete attached disks, snapshots,

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.275
object versions, logs, or backups.

00:01:14.275 --> 00:01:14.770
Conversely,

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:19.045
automatic cleanup can destroy required evidence or results if ownership is unclear.

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:24.045
Maintain an inventory that links storage to a project and owner.

00:01:24.045 --> 00:01:26.500
Privacy-conscious cloud GPU use means knowing where data enters, where copies remain,

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.420
who can access them, and how they are removed at the end of the workload.
